# D10(b) disjoint-sibling MOVE (IGNORED — pins an UNDECIDED policy question):
# `f(!m.a, !m.b)` moves two DISJOINT sibling sub-places. Today the move-tracker
# root-marks the whole DefId `m` on the first move, so the second `!m.b` trips
# E_UseAfterMove — an OVER-rejection relative to the D10(b) place-overlap rule
# (which treats disjoint siblings as non-overlapping). Whether this SHOULD accept
# is the Rust-style destructuring partial-move WIDENING, left UNDECIDED by the
# 2026-07-11 D10(a) ADDENDUM (decisions.md). D10(b) deliberately keeps (Move,Move)
# out of place-overlap, so this is NOT B1's to decide — the fixture documents the
# question so the current over-rejection is not silently blessed as canonical.
# Expected output flips to a POSITIVE (build+run) if the owner rules the widening
# in; ignored until then. See the TODO.md entry.
struct M:
    Vector[int] a
    Vector[int] b

void f(Vector[int] !x, Vector[int] !y):
    pass

void main():
    M m = M([1], [2])
    f(!m.a, !m.b)
